**Read:** [Introduction to Historical Geology — Estrada and contributing authors](../sources/Introduction-to-Historical-Geology.lingua#r_1JKSSECEDW0M9QG30TJ1D9KHZD), the complete fossil vocabulary, evidence, and historical applications; [International Stratigraphic Guide, abridged version — ICS](../sources/International-Stratigraphic-Guide.lingua#r_6NAYJE2KFXGRNCWP6TEY7AZE7J), Biostratigraphic Units in full; [Physical Geology, second edition — Steven Earle](../sources/Physical-Geology-Earle.lingua#r_5GWEYVQGR176TXMQT0S881ZSKX), chapter 8’s fossil-based dating.

Fossils can constrain biological history, environments, relative age, and correlation when identity, occurrence, preservation, and context are established. Distinguish an organism's inferred range from the observed range in a sampled record. Absence from one exposure may reflect sampling, preservation, ecology, or an actual temporal boundary.

Biostratigraphic inference uses assemblages, ranges, succession, and relationships under specified regional/global frameworks. An identification needs diagnostic evidence and appropriate expertise; a visually similar specimen does not automatically establish one species or age. Reworking can place an older fossil into younger sediment.

Body fossils, trace fossils, and other biological evidence answer different questions. Their spatial association and relationship with sedimentary structures can help interpret a deposit, while a single transported specimen can support a narrower conclusion. Tie interpretations to a documented specimen/observation and stratigraphic position.

**Work:** read a complete textbook fossil/correlation example and reconstruct its reasoning. Design an original hypothetical range/correlation table, including one sampling gap and one possible reworked occurrence. State which conclusions are supported and which need additional evidence.

Read the historical-geology fossil chapters in full and the guide's complete biostratigraphic section. Dedicated paleontology and biological evolution will deepen taxonomy, taphonomy, phylogeny, and research; general geology should already support critical use of fossil evidence rather than memorized index-fossil labels.

